    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/janis-cakste"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janis-cakste.jpg" alt="Jānis Čakste" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/janis-cakste">Jānis Čakste</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Janis_Cakste.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ei tiedossa, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic of Latvia was born in the turmoil at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Latvian national consciousness had grown since the national awakening of the mid-nineteenth century, and in 1915 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-riflemen">Latvian Riflemen</a> units were formed in the Russian army to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Riga against the Germans. After the Russian revolutions, German forces occupied all Latvian lands in 1917–1918, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat opened the way for independence. On 18 November 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-council-of-latvia">People's Council</a>, uniting the main Latvian parties, proclaimed the Republic of Latvia in Riga, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janis-cakste">Jānis Čakste</a> as chairman of the council and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a> as head of the Provisional Government. Within weeks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-socialist-soviet-republic">Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peteris-stucka">Pēteris Stučka</a> took Riga in January 1919, leaving the national government a foothold around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a>. In April 1919 German forces staged a coup there and installed a puppet cabinet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrievs-niedra">Andrievs Niedra</a>. Latvian and Estonian troops defeated the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-landeswehr">Landeswehr</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cesis">Battle of Cēsis</a> in June 1919, the national government returned to Riga, and the attack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-bermondt-avalov">Pavel Bermondt-Avalov</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-russian-volunteer-army">West Russian Volunteer Army</a> on the capital was repulsed in November. With Polish help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> was cleared of the Red Army in early 1920, and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised Latvian independence in a peace treaty signed in Riga on 11 August 1920.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jelgava"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jelgava.jpg" alt="Jelgava" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jelgava_winter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ivarsveilins, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Interwar Latvia had about two million people, roughly three-quarters of them ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, with substantial Russian, Jewish, German, Polish and Belarusian minorities, and a religious divide between Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and Zemgale and Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a>. War had devastated the country: Riga had lost much of its industry, evacuated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1915, and a large part of its population. The agrarian reform of 1920 expropriated most of the estates of the Baltic German nobility and created tens of thousands of new smallholdings, making independent farmers the backbone of society. Agriculture recovered and exports of butter, bacon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, mainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, sustained the economy. Industry revived more slowly, but the state electrical works VEF in Riga became known for radios and, from 1938, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minox">Minox</a> miniature camera. Minority schools in their own languages flourished in the 1920s. Soviet rule after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> brought collectivisation and heavy industrialisation, with factories such as the RAF minibus plant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a>, and large-scale immigration that reduced the Latvian share of the population to about 52 per cent by 1989. Since 1991 Latvia has become a market economy centred on Riga's port, transit trade, services, timber and information technology.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/christmas-battles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-battles.jpg" alt="Christmas Battles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/christmas-battles">Christmas Battles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziemassvetku_kaujas_1917.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Latvian military tradition began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-riflemen">Latvian Riflemen</a> of 1915–1917, whose costly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-battles">Christmas Battles</a> against the Germans in 1916–1917 became part of national memory; after 1917 many Red Latvian Riflemen served the Bolsheviks. The national army was formed during the War of Independence of 1918–1920, when the government fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-socialist-soviet-republic">Latvian Soviet republic</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-landeswehr">Baltic German Landeswehr</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">German Freikorps</a> units and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-russian-volunteer-army">Bermontians</a>. The defence of Riga against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-bermondt-avalov">Bermondt-Avalov</a> in October and November 1919, aided by Estonian armoured trains and Allied warships, is commemorated on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacplesis-day">Lāčplēsis Day</a>, 11 November. Interwar Latvia kept a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army under generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janis-balodis">Jānis Balodis</a>, a volunteer home guard, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizsargi">Aizsargi</a>, and a small navy with two French-built submarines, and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in 1923. It could not resist the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, which imposed bases in 1939 and occupied the country in June 1940 without fighting. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> were mobilised by both sides, notably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-legion">Latvian Legion</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waffen-ss">Waffen-SS</a>, and fighting continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland-pocket">Courland Pocket</a> until May 1945. Armed resistance by the national partisans lasted into the 1950s. The restored republic rebuilt its armed forces, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 2004, has hosted a Canadian-led NATO battlegroup at Ādaži since 2017 and reintroduced conscription in 2023.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-latvia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-latvia.jpg" alt="Constitution of Latvia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-latvia">Constitution of Latvia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latvijas_Republikas_Satversmes_publik%C4%81cija_1922._gada_30._j%C5%ABnija_%E2%80%9CVald%C4%ABbas_V%C4%93stnes%C4%AB%E2%80%9D.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-latvia">Satversme</a>, adopted by the Constituent Assembly on 15 February 1922, created a parliamentary democracy. The single-chamber <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeima">Saeima</a>, elected by proportional representation, chose the president, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janis-cakste">Jānis Čakste</a> served as the first president from 1922 until his death in 1927. Low thresholds produced a fragmented parliament with more than twenty parties, frequent changes of cabinet and growing frustration. On 15 May 1934 Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>, backed by the army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizsargi">Aizsargi</a>, suspended the Saeima and banned political parties, including the radical right <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perkonkrusts">Pērkonkrusts</a>. In 1936 he also assumed the presidency, ruling a paternalist authoritarian regime that promoted Latvian nationalism, corporatist chambers and state companies. After 1940 Latvia was governed as the Latvian Soviet Socialist Republic, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and other Western states never recognised the annexation, and Latvian diplomats abroad maintained the continuity of the state. The declaration of 4 May 1990 and the constitutional law of 21 August 1991 restored independence on that basis. The Satversme came fully back into force when the fifth Saeima met in July 1993 and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntis-ulmanis">Guntis Ulmanis</a> president. Citizenship was restored to pre-1940 citizens and their descendants, leaving many Soviet-era settlers as 'non-citizens' who could naturalise, an issue that remains politically sensitive.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis.jpg" alt="Kārlis Ulmanis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K._Ulmakis_LCCN2014712518_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bain News Service, publisher, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Latvia's experience mirrors the wider history of the small nations that emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1918 and were swallowed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1940. Its agrarian reform and its minority school system were notable experiments of the interwar period, and its slide from parliamentary instability to authoritarian rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Ulmanis</a> followed a pattern common in Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. The twin occupations had terrible human costs: Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> in June 1941 and March 1949 sent tens of thousands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, while under German rule about 70,000 Latvian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were murdered, many at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumbula-massacre">Rumbula</a> near Riga in 1941. These events remain central to Latvian memory and to debates on collaboration and historical responsibility. The principle of state continuity, upheld by Western non-recognition of the annexation, gave the national movement of the late 1980s a powerful legal argument, and Latvia's part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-way">Baltic Way</a> of 1989 and the barricades of January 1991 helped make the Baltic case a symbol of peaceful self-determination. Culturally, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Dance Festival tradition begun in 1873 and recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO</a>, and Riga's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> architecture, give Latvia a distinctive place in European heritage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact.jpg" alt="Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H27337,_Moskau,_Stalin_und_Ribbentrop_im_Kreml.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Template:Helmut Laux, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic was suspended rather than ended. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> forced Latvia to accept military bases in October 1939, delivered an ultimatum on 16 June 1940 and occupied the country the next day. Rigged elections produced a parliament that requested admission to the Soviet Union, and Latvia was annexed on 5 August 1940; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Ulmanis</a> was deported and died in Soviet custody in 1942. German occupation from 1941 to 1944 was followed by renewed Soviet rule. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>, environmental protests grew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-of-latvia">Popular Front of Latvia</a>, founded in 1988, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-way">Baltic Way</a> of 23 August 1989. The newly elected Supreme Council adopted the declaration on the restoration of independence on 4 May 1990, and after the failed August coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> full independence followed on 21 August 1991. The lats was reintroduced in 1993 and Russian troops withdrew in 1994. Under presidents including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaira-vike-freiberga">Vaira Vīķe-Freiberga</a>, Latvia joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004. The financial crisis of 2008–2009, which brought the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parex-bank">Parex Bank</a> and an international rescue loan, caused one of the deepest recessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but Latvia recovered and adopted the euro in 2014. Today it is a parliamentary republic of under two million people.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of Latvia illustrates the fate of the small nations created in 1918: it won independence in a many-sided war, fell to Soviet and Nazi occupation with heavy human losses, and used the doctrine of legal continuity and the peaceful Baltic independence movement to restore itself in 1990–1991.</em></p>

